{"id":"2026-07-10-57","date":"2026-07-10","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:30:49Z","observed_at":"2026-07-10T03:56:26Z","title":"Latent Memory Palace: Reasoning for Control as Autoregressive Variational Inference","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08724v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08724v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"Human decision-making is highly flexible -- some actions are taken immediately; others require longer deliberation. Language models have exhibited a similar capacity for adaptive \"reasoning.\" However, transferring this capability to continuous control policies has been challenging, as directly reasoning in language space may lack the granularity for spatial understanding and precise motions. In this work, we show that reasoning for control policies can emerge by organizing information in an autoregressive latent space reminiscent of a memory palace, where retrieval is iterative and adaptive. Our method, Latent Memory Palace (LMP), formulates reasoning as variational inference with an autoregressive latent distribution. We derive a latent-space reinforcement learning technique to tractably optimize its variational lower bound. The resulting policy, LMP-$π$, achieves strong empirical performance in simulation and real-world domains while exhibiting interpretable, adaptive allocation of test-time compute. We further show that the same framework yields a variable-length action tokenizer, LMP-$\\texttt{tok}$, which significantly improves the performance of downstream autoregressive policies. Together, these results present a new perspective on latent reasoning for control through the lens of variational inference.","codex_summary":"Control-policy reasoning research; lower AI PM actionability today.","display_summary":"Human decision-making is highly flexible -- some actions are taken immediately; others require longer deliberation. Language models have exhibited a similar capacity for adaptive \"reasoning.\" However, transferring this capability to continuous control policies has been challenging, as directly reasoning in language space may lack the granularity for spatial understanding and precise motions. In this work, we show that reasoning for control policies can emerge by organizing information in an autoregressive latent space reminiscent of a memory palace, where retrieval is iterative and adaptive. Our method, Latent Memory Palace (LMP), formulates reasoning as variational inference with an autoregressive latent distribution. We derive a latent-space reinforcement learning technique to tractably optimize its variational lower bound. The resulting policy, LMP-$π$, achieves strong empirical performance in simulation and real-world domains while exhibiting interpretable, adaptive allocation of test-time compute. We further show that the same framework yields a variable-length action tokenizer, LMP-$\\texttt{tok}$, which significantly improves the performance of downstream autoregressive policies. Together, these results present a new perspective on latent reasoning for control through the lens of variational inference.","summary":"Human decision-making is highly flexible -- some actions are taken immediately; others require longer deliberation. Language models have exhibited a similar capacity for adaptive \"reasoning.\" However, transferring this capability to continuous control policies has been challenging, as directly reasoning in language space may lack the granularity for spatial understanding and precise motions. In this work, we show that reasoning for control policies can emerge by organizing information in an autoregressive latent space reminiscent of a memory palace, where retrieval is iterative and adaptive. Our method, Latent Memory Palace (LMP), formulates reasoning as variational inference with an autoregressive latent distribution. We derive a latent-space reinforcement learning technique to tractably optimize its variational lower bound. The resulting policy, LMP-$π$, achieves strong empirical performance in simulation and real-world domains while exhibiting interpretable, adaptive allocation of test-time compute. We further show that the same framework yields a variable-length action tokenizer, LMP-$\\texttt{tok}$, which significantly improves the performance of downstream autoregressive policies. Together, these results present a new perspective on latent reasoning for control through the lens of variational inference.","why_it_matters":"Control-policy reasoning research; lower AI PM actionability today.","priority":"P2","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"paper_candidate","candidate_pool":"paper_candidate","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"paper_candidate","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08724","duplicate_status":"new","duplicate_window_status":"new","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"","practice_fit":"low","reason":"Control-policy reasoning research; lower AI PM actionability today.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"","status":"watching","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"low","target_role_signal":"AI PM / eval / agent workflow","proof_artifact":"paper reading note","validation_window":"one week","interview_story_angle":"Control-policy reasoning research; lower AI PM actionability today.","next_proof_action":"Move to weekly paper review if manually confirmed.","reading_category":""}
{"id":"2026-07-10-58","date":"2026-07-10","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:26:43Z","observed_at":"2026-07-10T03:56:26Z","title":"Deep Learning for Joint Narrowband Interference Cancellation and Soft Demodulation in OFDM Systems","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08717v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08717v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"Narrowband interference (NBI) severely degrades orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) systems by corrupting subcarriers and rendering classical soft demodulation ineffective. Conventional compressed-sensing (CS) mitigation exhibits high sequential latency and leaves structured, non-Gaussian residuals that cause log-likelihood ratio (LLR) unreliability, decoder saturation, and severe error floors when employing classical Gaussian demappers. We resolve this pipeline mismatch using a unified deep learning framework for joint NBI cancellation and robust soft demodulation. First, NBI-CNet employs a physics-informed convolutional architecture to estimate NBI parameters and remove multi-tone interference in a single forward pass. Without requiring prior knowledge of the active interferer count, NBI-CNet reduces computational complexity by up to 60% ($N{=}2048, Q{=}64$) compared to the state-of-the-art EOMP-IDS algorithm. Second, LLR-CNet acts as a structural whitener by mapping non-Gaussian post-mitigation residuals onto well-calibrated soft metrics. Simulations demonstrate that this joint framework eliminates the error floors inherent to traditional baselines across dense grids. Under severe interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}{-}10$ dB), the pipeline operates within a $0.2$ to $0.5$ dB SNR margin of the optimal iterative baseline at a target block error rate (BLER) of $10^{-4}$. Under mild interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}10$ dB) with heavy spectral overlap ($Q{=}12$), where classical greedy algorithms erroneously subtract valid data components and corrupt the payload, NBI-CNet avoids signal-peak confusion to deliver a coding gain exceeding $3$ dB. Finally, the architecture circumvents the $2{\\times}10^{-4}$ error floor triggered by interferer-estimation errors, while its scale-invariant design enables robust generalization across arbitrary FFT sizes without retraining.","codex_summary":"Low-priority research context for today.","display_summary":"Narrowband interference (NBI) severely degrades orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) systems by corrupting subcarriers and rendering classical soft demodulation ineffective. Conventional compressed-sensing (CS) mitigation exhibits high sequential latency and leaves structured, non-Gaussian residuals that cause log-likelihood ratio (LLR) unreliability, decoder saturation, and severe error floors when employing classical Gaussian demappers. We resolve this pipeline mismatch using a unified deep learning framework for joint NBI cancellation and robust soft demodulation. First, NBI-CNet employs a physics-informed convolutional architecture to estimate NBI parameters and remove multi-tone interference in a single forward pass. Without requiring prior knowledge of the active interferer count, NBI-CNet reduces computational complexity by up to 60% ($N{=}2048, Q{=}64$) compared to the state-of-the-art EOMP-IDS algorithm. Second, LLR-CNet acts as a structural whitener by mapping non-Gaussian post-mitigation residuals onto well-calibrated soft metrics. Simulations demonstrate that this joint framework eliminates the error floors inherent to traditional baselines across dense grids. Under severe interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}{-}10$ dB), the pipeline operates within a $0.2$ to $0.5$ dB SNR margin of the optimal iterative baseline at a target block error rate (BLER) of $10^{-4}$. Under mild interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}10$ dB) with heavy spectral overlap ($Q{=}12$), where classical greedy algorithms erroneously subtract valid data components and corrupt the payload, NBI-CNet avoids signal-peak confusion to deliver a coding gain exceeding $3$ dB. Finally, the architecture circumvents the $2{\\times}10^{-4}$ error floor triggered by interferer-estimation errors, while its scale-invariant design enables robust generalization across arbitrary FFT sizes without retraining.","summary":"Narrowband interference (NBI) severely degrades orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) systems by corrupting subcarriers and rendering classical soft demodulation ineffective. Conventional compressed-sensing (CS) mitigation exhibits high sequential latency and leaves structured, non-Gaussian residuals that cause log-likelihood ratio (LLR) unreliability, decoder saturation, and severe error floors when employing classical Gaussian demappers. We resolve this pipeline mismatch using a unified deep learning framework for joint NBI cancellation and robust soft demodulation. First, NBI-CNet employs a physics-informed convolutional architecture to estimate NBI parameters and remove multi-tone interference in a single forward pass. Without requiring prior knowledge of the active interferer count, NBI-CNet reduces computational complexity by up to 60% ($N{=}2048, Q{=}64$) compared to the state-of-the-art EOMP-IDS algorithm. Second, LLR-CNet acts as a structural whitener by mapping non-Gaussian post-mitigation residuals onto well-calibrated soft metrics. Simulations demonstrate that this joint framework eliminates the error floors inherent to traditional baselines across dense grids. Under severe interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}{-}10$ dB), the pipeline operates within a $0.2$ to $0.5$ dB SNR margin of the optimal iterative baseline at a target block error rate (BLER) of $10^{-4}$. Under mild interference ($\\text{SIR}{=}10$ dB) with heavy spectral overlap ($Q{=}12$), where classical greedy algorithms erroneously subtract valid data components and corrupt the payload, NBI-CNet avoids signal-peak confusion to deliver a coding gain exceeding $3$ dB. Finally, the architecture circumvents the $2{\\times}10^{-4}$ error floor triggered by interferer-estimation errors, while its scale-invariant design enables robust generalization across arbitrary FFT sizes without retraining.","why_it_matters":"Low-priority research context for today.","priority":"archive","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"archive","candidate_pool":"archive","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"archive","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08717","duplicate_status":"new","duplicate_window_status":"new","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"","practice_fit":"low","reason":"Fresh arXiv supplement kept for completeness; low direct fit with today AI PM/full-stack focus.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"","status":"inbox","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"low","target_role_signal":"research context","proof_artifact":"","validation_window":"","interview_story_angle":"","next_proof_action":"Archive unless a future theme matches.","reading_category":""}
{"id":"2026-07-10-59","date":"2026-07-10","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:26:28Z","observed_at":"2026-07-10T03:56:26Z","title":"Remember When It Matters: Proactive Memory Agent for Long-Horizon Agents","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08716v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08716v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"In long-horizon tasks, decision-relevant state is often scattered across an expanding trajectory, while the action agent must surface it and act. As trajectories grow, task requirements, environment facts, prior attempts, diagnoses, and open subgoals can be buried in the context window or pushed beyond it, failing to influence decisions when needed. We call this failure mode \"behavioral state decay\". We study memory as an active intervention mechanism rather than passive retrieval. A separate memory agent runs alongside an unmodified action agent, updating a structured memory bank from the recent trajectory and deciding whether to inject a memory-grounded reminder or remain silent. The module is plug-and-play with frontier action agents and existing agent harnesses. Across Terminal-Bench 2.0 and $τ^2$-Bench, it improves pass@1 for both weaker and stronger action agents, with gains of +8.3 pp on Terminal-Bench and +6.8 pp on $τ^2$-Bench. Ablations show that selective intervention outperforms passive bank exposure, always-on injection, advisor-only guidance, and general retrieval. As an early step toward open-weight memory policies, we train Qwen3.5-27B on SETA using SFT and GRPO, improving validation reward and achieving partial transfer to Terminal-Bench.","codex_summary":"Long-horizon memory failure is a core agent product reliability gap.","display_summary":"In long-horizon tasks, decision-relevant state is often scattered across an expanding trajectory, while the action agent must surface it and act. As trajectories grow, task requirements, environment facts, prior attempts, diagnoses, and open subgoals can be buried in the context window or pushed beyond it, failing to influence decisions when needed. We call this failure mode \"behavioral state decay\". We study memory as an active intervention mechanism rather than passive retrieval. A separate memory agent runs alongside an unmodified action agent, updating a structured memory bank from the recent trajectory and deciding whether to inject a memory-grounded reminder or remain silent. The module is plug-and-play with frontier action agents and existing agent harnesses. Across Terminal-Bench 2.0 and $τ^2$-Bench, it improves pass@1 for both weaker and stronger action agents, with gains of +8.3 pp on Terminal-Bench and +6.8 pp on $τ^2$-Bench. Ablations show that selective intervention outperforms passive bank exposure, always-on injection, advisor-only guidance, and general retrieval. As an early step toward open-weight memory policies, we train Qwen3.5-27B on SETA using SFT and GRPO, improving validation reward and achieving partial transfer to Terminal-Bench.","summary":"In long-horizon tasks, decision-relevant state is often scattered across an expanding trajectory, while the action agent must surface it and act. As trajectories grow, task requirements, environment facts, prior attempts, diagnoses, and open subgoals can be buried in the context window or pushed beyond it, failing to influence decisions when needed. We call this failure mode \"behavioral state decay\". We study memory as an active intervention mechanism rather than passive retrieval. A separate memory agent runs alongside an unmodified action agent, updating a structured memory bank from the recent trajectory and deciding whether to inject a memory-grounded reminder or remain silent. The module is plug-and-play with frontier action agents and existing agent harnesses. Across Terminal-Bench 2.0 and $τ^2$-Bench, it improves pass@1 for both weaker and stronger action agents, with gains of +8.3 pp on Terminal-Bench and +6.8 pp on $τ^2$-Bench. Ablations show that selective intervention outperforms passive bank exposure, always-on injection, advisor-only guidance, and general retrieval. As an early step toward open-weight memory policies, we train Qwen3.5-27B on SETA using SFT and GRPO, improving validation reward and achieving partial transfer to Terminal-Bench.","why_it_matters":"Long-horizon memory failure is a core agent product reliability gap.","priority":"P1","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"candidate","suggested_pool":"paper_candidate","candidate_pool":"paper_candidate","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"paper_candidate","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08716","duplicate_status":"new","duplicate_window_status":"new","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"","practice_fit":"high","reason":"Long-horizon memory failure is a core agent product reliability gap.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"","status":"watching","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"medium","target_role_signal":"AI PM / eval / agent workflow","proof_artifact":"paper reading note","validation_window":"one week","interview_story_angle":"Long-horizon memory failure is a core agent product reliability gap.","next_proof_action":"Move to weekly paper review if manually confirmed.","reading_category":""}
{"id":"2026-07-10-60","date":"2026-07-10","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:22:35Z","observed_at":"2026-07-10T03:56:26Z","title":"LTM: Large-scale Terrain Model for Wildfire-prone Landscapes","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08711v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08711v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"Accurate 3D terrain maps are essential for emergency response when assessing wildfire hazards. However, wildfire-prone regions often span vast areas where conventional reconstruction methods underperform. Airborne LiDAR systems provide high-resolution terrain data, but they are expensive and infrequently updated. Image-based methods offer a lower-cost alternative, but struggle due to sparse visual features and limited image overlap. We propose a multi-modal reconstruction framework leveraging outdated Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) as geometric priors for image-based 3D reconstruction. Our key innovation is physics-based pixel-pixel alignment between images and DEM data, dramatically reducing computational complexity by eliminating expensive feature matching procedures. To validate our approach, we developed a large-terrain simulator based on a real wildfire-prone area, generating realistic images enabling a comprehensive evaluation. Given posed images and legacy DEMs, our method produces high-fidelity depth maps while maintaining real-time performance. We find significant improvements in reconstruction accuracy and computational efficiency over existing techniques, offering a scalable solution for wildfire response.","codex_summary":"Low-priority research context for today.","display_summary":"Accurate 3D terrain maps are essential for emergency response when assessing wildfire hazards. However, wildfire-prone regions often span vast areas where conventional reconstruction methods underperform. Airborne LiDAR systems provide high-resolution terrain data, but they are expensive and infrequently updated. Image-based methods offer a lower-cost alternative, but struggle due to sparse visual features and limited image overlap. We propose a multi-modal reconstruction framework leveraging outdated Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) as geometric priors for image-based 3D reconstruction. Our key innovation is physics-based pixel-pixel alignment between images and DEM data, dramatically reducing computational complexity by eliminating expensive feature matching procedures. To validate our approach, we developed a large-terrain simulator based on a real wildfire-prone area, generating realistic images enabling a comprehensive evaluation. Given posed images and legacy DEMs, our method produces high-fidelity depth maps while maintaining real-time performance. We find significant improvements in reconstruction accuracy and computational efficiency over existing techniques, offering a scalable solution for wildfire response.","summary":"Accurate 3D terrain maps are essential for emergency response when assessing wildfire hazards. However, wildfire-prone regions often span vast areas where conventional reconstruction methods underperform. Airborne LiDAR systems provide high-resolution terrain data, but they are expensive and infrequently updated. Image-based methods offer a lower-cost alternative, but struggle due to sparse visual features and limited image overlap. We propose a multi-modal reconstruction framework leveraging outdated Digital Elevation Models (DEMs) as geometric priors for image-based 3D reconstruction. Our key innovation is physics-based pixel-pixel alignment between images and DEM data, dramatically reducing computational complexity by eliminating expensive feature matching procedures. To validate our approach, we developed a large-terrain simulator based on a real wildfire-prone area, generating realistic images enabling a comprehensive evaluation. Given posed images and legacy DEMs, our method produces high-fidelity depth maps while maintaining real-time performance. We find significant improvements in reconstruction accuracy and computational efficiency over existing techniques, offering a scalable solution for wildfire response.","why_it_matters":"Low-priority research context for today.","priority":"archive","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"archive","candidate_pool":"archive","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"archive","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08711","duplicate_status":"new","duplicate_window_status":"new","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"","practice_fit":"low","reason":"Fresh arXiv supplement kept for completeness; low direct fit with today AI PM/full-stack focus.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"","status":"inbox","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"low","target_role_signal":"research context","proof_artifact":"","validation_window":"","interview_story_angle":"","next_proof_action":"Archive unless a future theme matches.","reading_category":""}
{"id":"2026-07-10-61","date":"2026-07-10","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:09:20Z","observed_at":"2026-07-10T03:56:26Z","title":"MPFlow: Learning Budgeted Max-Flow Optimization on the Lightning Network with Deep Graph Reinforcement Learning","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08703v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08703v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"We address liquidity placement in the Bitcoin Lightning Network (LN): given a fixed budget, which channels should a node open to maximize its routing capacity? We cast this as a budget-constrained combinatorial optimization problem on graphs, selecting $k$ edge additions that maximize $s$--$t$ max-flow, a theory-grounded measure of routing capacity, and solve it with graph reinforcement learning. Our lightweight agent combines a message-passing policy network with proximal policy optimization (PPO) and action masking, and is trained under a hub-exclusion curriculum: the network's top hubs are removed from training subgraphs, forcing the policy to learn capacity-aware placement rather than hub attachment. In extensive experiments on real Lightning Network snapshots, our method consistently outperforms strong heuristic baselines on the max-flow objective across multiple seeds and unseen graphs. The agent has been deployed in production for peer recommendations, executing 4640 channel-open decisions that cumulatively allocate 267.3 BTC over $16 million across 30 managed nodes.","codex_summary":"Low-priority research context for today.","display_summary":"We address liquidity placement in the Bitcoin Lightning Network (LN): given a fixed budget, which channels should a node open to maximize its routing capacity? We cast this as a budget-constrained combinatorial optimization problem on graphs, selecting $k$ edge additions that maximize $s$--$t$ max-flow, a theory-grounded measure of routing capacity, and solve it with graph reinforcement learning. Our lightweight agent combines a message-passing policy network with proximal policy optimization (PPO) and action masking, and is trained under a hub-exclusion curriculum: the network's top hubs are removed from training subgraphs, forcing the policy to learn capacity-aware placement rather than hub attachment. In extensive experiments on real Lightning Network snapshots, our method consistently outperforms strong heuristic baselines on the max-flow objective across multiple seeds and unseen graphs. The agent has been deployed in production for peer recommendations, executing 4640 channel-open decisions that cumulatively allocate 267.3 BTC over $16 million across 30 managed nodes.","summary":"We address liquidity placement in the Bitcoin Lightning Network (LN): given a fixed budget, which channels should a node open to maximize its routing capacity? We cast this as a budget-constrained combinatorial optimization problem on graphs, selecting $k$ edge additions that maximize $s$--$t$ max-flow, a theory-grounded measure of routing capacity, and solve it with graph reinforcement learning. Our lightweight agent combines a message-passing policy network with proximal policy optimization (PPO) and action masking, and is trained under a hub-exclusion curriculum: the network's top hubs are removed from training subgraphs, forcing the policy to learn capacity-aware placement rather than hub attachment. In extensive experiments on real Lightning Network snapshots, our method consistently outperforms strong heuristic baselines on the max-flow objective across multiple seeds and unseen graphs. The agent has been deployed in production for peer recommendations, executing 4640 channel-open decisions that cumulatively allocate 267.3 BTC over $16 million across 30 managed nodes.","why_it_matters":"Low-priority research context for today.","priority":"archive","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"archive","candidate_pool":"archive","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"archive","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08703","duplicate_status":"new","duplicate_window_status":"new","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"","practice_fit":"low","reason":"Fresh arXiv supplement kept for completeness; low direct fit with today AI PM/full-stack focus.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"","status":"inbox","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"low","target_role_signal":"research context","proof_artifact":"","validation_window":"","interview_story_angle":"","next_proof_action":"Archive unless a future theme matches.","reading_category":""}
