{"id":"2026-07-11-33","date":"2026-07-11","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:51:50Z","observed_at":"2026-07-11T09:27:49+08:00","title":"SLORR: Simple and Efficient In-Training Low-Rank Regularization","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08754v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08754v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"Low-rank factorization is widely used to compress neural networks, but modern models are often not naturally amenable to aggressive factorization without significant accuracy loss. Existing training-time low-rank regularizers can improve compressibility, but they often require SVDs of large weight matrices, modify the model architecture (introducing additional trainable parameters), or rely on stateful cached quantities. To address these limitations, we introduce SLORR, a simple, stateless, and architecture-preserving framework for in-training low-rank regularization, instantiated with two main variants based on the Hoyer sparsity metric and the nuclear norm. SLORR directly regularizes the original weight matrices using GPU-friendly approximations for the forward and backward passes of the regularizers, for which we provide approximation guarantees. We first evaluate SLORR on ImageNet-1K across short-horizon continued training of ResNet-50, ViT-B/16, and ViT-L/16, and pretraining of ResNet-18, where SLORR induces compressibility while introducing less than 8% training overhead. We further evaluate SLORR-Hoyer in LLM pretraining at 135M and 560M scales: SLORR-trained compressed models preserve performance substantially better than unregularized models while adding less than 1% average training overhead.","codex_summary":"Compression research is relevant but lower actionability today.","display_summary":"Low-rank factorization is widely used to compress neural networks, but modern models are often not naturally amenable to aggressive factorization without significant accuracy loss. Existing training-time low-rank regularizers can improve compressibility, but they often require SVDs of large weight matrices, modify the model architecture (introducing additional trainable parameters), or rely on stateful cached quantities. To address these limitations, we introduce SLORR, a simple, stateless, and architecture-preserving framework for in-training low-rank regularization, instantiated with two main variants based on the Hoyer sparsity metric and the nuclear norm. SLORR directly regularizes the original weight matrices using GPU-friendly approximations for the forward and backward passes of the regularizers, for which we provide approximation guarantees. We first evaluate SLORR on ImageNet-1K across short-horizon continued training of ResNet-50, ViT-B/16, and ViT-L/16, and pretraining of ResNet-18, where SLORR induces compressibility while introducing less than 8% training overhead. We further evaluate SLORR-Hoyer in LLM pretraining at 135M and 560M scales: SLORR-trained compressed models preserve performance substantially better than unregularized models while adding less than 1% average training overhead.","summary":"Low-rank factorization is widely used to compress neural networks, but modern models are often not naturally amenable to aggressive factorization without significant accuracy loss. Existing training-time low-rank regularizers can improve compressibility, but they often require SVDs of large weight matrices, modify the model architecture (introducing additional trainable parameters), or rely on stateful cached quantities. To address these limitations, we introduce SLORR, a simple, stateless, and architecture-preserving framework for in-training low-rank regularization, instantiated with two main variants based on the Hoyer sparsity metric and the nuclear norm. SLORR directly regularizes the original weight matrices using GPU-friendly approximations for the forward and backward passes of the regularizers, for which we provide approximation guarantees. We first evaluate SLORR on ImageNet-1K across short-horizon continued training of ResNet-50, ViT-B/16, and ViT-L/16, and pretraining of ResNet-18, where SLORR induces compressibility while introducing less than 8% training overhead. We further evaluate SLORR-Hoyer in LLM pretraining at 135M and 560M scales: SLORR-trained compressed models preserve performance substantially better than unregularized models while adding less than 1% average training overhead.","why_it_matters":"Compression research is relevant but lower actionability today.","priority":"P2","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"archive","candidate_pool":"archive","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"archive","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08754","duplicate_status":"duplicate_suppressed","duplicate_window_status":"duplicate_in_7d","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"7 天内已在 2026-07-10 记录同一链接或同一 canonical_key；本次保留结构化状态，不进入主动阅读包。","practice_fit":"low","reason":"Compression research is relevant but lower actionability today.","read_reason":"","focus_direction":"","known_facts":"","open_questions":"","url_status":"verification_unavailable","url_checked_at":"","requires_manual_review":true,"next_action":"人工确认候选池路由","status":"suppressed","tags":[],"interview_relevance":"medium","target_role_signal":"","proof_artifact":"","validation_window":"","interview_story_angle":"","next_proof_action":"","reading_category":""}
{"id":"2026-07-11-34","date":"2026-07-11","published_at":"2026-07-09T17:49:14Z","observed_at":"2026-07-11T09:27:49+08:00","title":"Using AI-based Learning Assistants in Higher Education: A Large-Scale Descriptive Analysis","original_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08748v1","source_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.08748v1","source_origin":"primary","source_name":"arXiv Export API","source_type":"paper","source_tier":"Tier 1","source_role":"daily_discovery","review_cadence":"daily","source_mix_note":"arxiv_export_supplement: fresh Atom entry; scanned for paper-candidate or knowledge-gap fit","original_format":"paper","aihot_id":"","aihot_summary":"","aihot_summary_status":"not_applicable","discovery_summary":"In this study, we present a large-scale descriptive analysis of the use of an AI-based learning assistant (Syntea) in higher education. Based on objective log data from 77,543 students enrolled in distance studies, we examine usage patterns across gender, age group, study cluster, degree, and study mode. To date, existing research on educational chatbots has largely relied on comparatively small samples and self-reported survey data, while large-scale evidence on actual usage behavior remains limited. Our findings show that Syntea is already embedded in the study routines of many learners, but that usage differs across demographic and structural contexts. By identifying these patterns, our study provides an empirical basis for the further development of AI-based learning support and contributes a large-scale analysis of educational chatbot usage in higher education.","codex_summary":"Large-scale learning assistant logs can inform education AI usage metrics.","display_summary":"In this study, we present a large-scale descriptive analysis of the use of an AI-based learning assistant (Syntea) in higher education. Based on objective log data from 77,543 students enrolled in distance studies, we examine usage patterns across gender, age group, study cluster, degree, and study mode. To date, existing research on educational chatbots has largely relied on comparatively small samples and self-reported survey data, while large-scale evidence on actual usage behavior remains limited. Our findings show that Syntea is already embedded in the study routines of many learners, but that usage differs across demographic and structural contexts. By identifying these patterns, our study provides an empirical basis for the further development of AI-based learning support and contributes a large-scale analysis of educational chatbot usage in higher education.","summary":"In this study, we present a large-scale descriptive analysis of the use of an AI-based learning assistant (Syntea) in higher education. Based on objective log data from 77,543 students enrolled in distance studies, we examine usage patterns across gender, age group, study cluster, degree, and study mode. To date, existing research on educational chatbots has largely relied on comparatively small samples and self-reported survey data, while large-scale evidence on actual usage behavior remains limited. Our findings show that Syntea is already embedded in the study routines of many learners, but that usage differs across demographic and structural contexts. By identifying these patterns, our study provides an empirical basis for the further development of AI-based learning support and contributes a large-scale analysis of educational chatbot usage in higher education.","why_it_matters":"Large-scale learning assistant logs can inform education AI usage metrics.","priority":"P2","confidence":"medium","reading_pack_status":"not_selected","suggested_pool":"archive","candidate_pool":"archive","human_status":"pending","final_pool":"archive","canonical_key":"arxiv.org/abs/2607.08748","duplicate_status":"duplicate_suppressed","duplicate_window_status":"duplicate_in_7d","freshness_status":"fresh_24h","novelty_reason":"7 天内已在 2026-07-10 记录同一链接或同一 canonical_key；本次保留结构化状态，不进入主动阅读包。","practi